issues.problematic_code_snippet:
/* Validate that stored-key to key conversion works correctly */
static int validateStoredKeyConversion(dict *d, const void *key __stored_key) {
    const void *extracted = dictStoredKey2Key(d, key);
    if (d->type->keyFromStoredKey) {
        return extracted != NULL;
    }
    return extracted == key;
}

/* Before the key is released, obtain the link to
 * ensure we can safely access and update the key. */
const void *key = dictStoredKey2Key(d, ptr);
link = dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL);
serverAssert(link);

/* Setting key of existing dictEntry (newItem == 0)*/

if (*link == NULL) {
    *link = dictFindLink(d, addedKey, NULL);
    assert(*link != NULL);
}

findings.body:
In `activeDefragHfieldAndUpdateRef` the PR computes `const void *key = dictStoredKey2Key(d, ptr);` but then calls `dictFindLink(d, ptr, NULL)`, passing the stored object. The converted `key` is never used, which is also why the compiler will warn about an unused variable.

Today this still works, because hfield dicts have no `keyFromStoredKey` and the conversion is the identity. The code is still wrong, though. It defeats the point of the new API: the next time someone gives a dict a non-identity `keyFromStoredKey`, this defrag path will silently search with the wrong key and hit `serverAssert(link)`.

::diff{file="src/defrag.c" lines="278-284"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The unused `key` variable is what makes this look intentional. It is not. Use `key` in the `dictFindLink` call.

::suggestion{file="src/defrag.c" lines="280-281"}
const void *key = dictStoredKey2Key(d, ptr);
link = dictFindLink(d, key, NULL);

On the `newItem == 0` path with a NULL `*link`, `dictSetKeyAtLink` now calls `dictFindLink(d, addedKey, NULL)`. `addedKey` is the stored-key form, either the original object or its `keyDup` copy. `dictFindLink` expects a lookup key. The newItem=1 branch just above does the conversion correctly with `dictStoredKey2Key(d, key)`, so this branch is inconsistent with it.

For the keyspace dict, `keyDup` is NULL and `addedKey` is the kvobj. `dictFindLink` then hashes the kvobj as if it were an sds, so the bucket search misses or lands in the wrong bucket. For a dict whose `keyDup` is set, the duplicated object is passed to a lookup API in the same way. The old code had the same mistake with `key`, and the PR changed the argument without fixing the conversion.

I could not find a current caller that reaches this branch, since existing callers pass a non-NULL link. It is still a latent trap in the code this PR is meant to clean up.

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="909-918"}

::callout{type="info"}
I could not find a current caller that passes a NULL `*link` with newItem=0, so this is latent rather than a live bug.

::suggestion{file="src/dict.c" lines="913-916"}
    if (*link == NULL) {
        *link = dictFindLink(d, dictStoredKey2Key(d, addedKey), NULL);
        assert(*link != NULL);
    }

`validateStoredKeyConversion` is defined as a static function in `dict.c`, but nothing calls it. The PR leaves it in place. The compiler will warn about an unused static function (`-Wunused-function`). The build does not use `-Werror` for that warning, so it does not break the build. It is still dead code in a hot file. Its logic also does not add a check. It returns `extracted != NULL`, which is weaker than any real validation.

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="101-108"}

Delete the function.

The new comment on `dictGetHash` says the compiler inlines it for internal calls and that this was verified with -O3. That claim cannot be checked from the code, and it does not explain why `dictGetHash` exists. Callers now get hashing with no stored-key handling, and the rest of dict.c uses `dictGetHash` directly.

The comment will go stale the next time the compiler or flags change. Describe the contract instead: the function hashes a lookup key with `type->hashFunction`.

::diff{file="src/dict.c" lines="1807-1810"}

After this PR, `dictSdsCompareKV` takes two sds values and never touches a kvobj. Its only difference from the existing `dictSdsKeyCompare` is the `dictCmpCache` length caching. The name still says `KV`, which suggests it unpacks a kvobj. A reader has to compare the two to see why both exist.

Keep the cached variant, since it saves repeated `sdslen` on each probe, but rename it to say what it does, for example `dictSdsKeyCompareCached`. Add a one-line comment explaining why it differs from `dictSdsKeyCompare`.

::diff{file="src/server.c" lines="321-333"}

The comment on `keyFromStoredKey` in `dict.h` says it is "Relevant only for no_value=1 dicts." The code does not enforce that. `dictStoredKey2Key` runs for every dict whose type sets the callback, and the stored-key path in `dictAddRaw` and the lookups applies no `no_value` check. The comment and the code disagree, so a future author may assume a no_value=0 dict will ignore the callback when it will not.

Either drop the restriction from the comment, or assert it in `_dictInit`.

::diff{file="src/dict.h" lines="127-134"}

Removing `dictUseStoredKeyApi` also removes a class of bug. The old flag was set and cleared around each call. An early return or an error path between the set and the clear would leave the dict in stored-key mode for later calls. The new design derives the mode from the dict type, which is stateless per call.

I checked the removed call sites in rdb.c and t_hash.c, and every set had a matching clear on each path. The change is therefore a simplification and not a fix for a bug that was live today.

::diff{file="src/rdb.c" lines="2234-2240"}

I checked that dropping `isSdsField` from `hashTypeDelete` does not change behaviour. The flag only affected dicts in stored-key mode. Hash dicts had no stored-key callbacks, so the old code took the same path either way. The only dbDictType user is the keyspace, which never calls `hashTypeDelete`.

`onFieldExpire` passes `entryGetField(e)`, which is an sds, so the new single signature is correct for that call site too. The other callers in t_hash.c, module.c, and the HGETDEL and HDEL commands all pass sds fields.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="3500-3504"}
